WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:03.760
At the time it was made, Saw 7 was to be&nbsp;
the final chapter of the Saw franchise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:08.160
and in honor of the series it would bring&nbsp;
back old characters, old locations, old music,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:13.040
old tricks and old traps. But I just want to know&nbsp;
what happened to the old hot dog guy from Saw 2?

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.400
We’re gonna get into all the Saw series&nbsp;
callbacks that are in this movie (and there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.400 --> 00:00:19.920
are a lot of them), but first, let’s talk&nbsp;
about something new. For the first time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:19.920 --> 00:00:23.680
the opening trap takes place out in public&nbsp;
during the day rather than in a dark,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.680 --> 00:00:27.440
abandoned cellar of some kind. The characters&nbsp;
find themselves in a display window for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:32.240
a store called Welthammer. Judging by the&nbsp;
style, this is 1000% where FoundFlix shops,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.240 --> 00:00:35.440
and in one wide shot we see their&nbsp;
slogan, “Renovating Your Life”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.440 --> 00:00:40.080
which I think fits nicely with the goal of Jigsaw,&nbsp;
to rehabilitate the lives of his test subjects.

00:00:40.080 --> 00:00:44.160
He was way ahead of his time, teaching&nbsp;
young men the dangers of getting simped.

00:00:44.160 --> 00:00:50.800
“The same woman has played each of you for a fool,&nbsp;
manipulating your love for her and causing nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:53.280
but pain.”
“She is toxic.”

00:00:53.280 --> 00:00:55.760
Now I saw James’s Kill Count, and upon mention of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.760 --> 00:01:00.080
the word toxic he put a Britney Spears&nbsp;
soundbite so uh… I’m gonna one up him.

00:01:00.080 --> 00:01:02.480
🎵 Toxicity - System of a Down 🎵

00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:06.000
And honestly, this is probably the&nbsp;
happiest ending of any Saw trap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:10.443
and Hoffman retires to his new hideout,&nbsp;
which contains a variety of secrets.

00:01:14.560 --> 00:01:19.520
Welcome to Things You Missed. I tend to refer&nbsp;
to the original 7 Saw movies as “The Saw Saga”,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:24.080
so here we are at the end of it. The Saw movies&nbsp;
were longtime kings of the October box office.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.080 --> 00:01:29.360
There was even a marketing phrase that went,&nbsp;
“if it’s Halloween, it must be Saw.” But Saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:29.360 --> 00:01:34.720
6 was actually beaten in 2009 by Paranormal&nbsp;
Activity, and Saw 6’s director, Kevin Gruetert&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.720 --> 00:01:38.800
was supposed to direct Paranormal Activity&nbsp;
2, which would go head to head with Saw 7.

00:01:38.800 --> 00:01:42.160
So the studio said… “You know what?&nbsp;
You’re contractually obligated to do&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:44.320
another movie for us, you can’t do Paranormal 2.”

00:01:44.320 --> 00:01:48.880
So Kevin Gruetert said “fine, but I’m gonna change&nbsp;
a bunch of stuff last minute and it’s gonna suck.”

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:53.840
“Fine.” “FINE!” “..........But&nbsp;
were shooting it in 3D.”

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.160
Hoffman’s hideout is filled with the stuff we’ve&nbsp;
come to expect to find in a Jigsaw workshop:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:04.720
Billys, piggies, and mannequins. This mannequin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.720 --> 00:02:08.240
could be prototype of the harness&nbsp;
that Dina wears in the opening trap.

00:02:08.240 --> 00:02:11.680
I’ve pointed out clues about the location&nbsp;
of the Saw saga throughout this series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.680 --> 00:02:15.440
and most evidence has pointed to the setting&nbsp;
being a fictional version of Newark, NJ,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.440 --> 00:02:19.760
while others seem to point towards New York City.&nbsp;
The newscast mentions a storm hitting the East&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.760 --> 00:02:25.040
Coast, but Hoffman’s drivers license looks more&nbsp;
inspired by a New York ID, unlike the license&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:29.280
plate which seem more New Jersey. One thing’s&nbsp;
for sure, that’s gonna be a disgusting omelet.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:31.440
Also, the money he’s putting in his bag at the end&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.560
of the movie says Bank of New York&nbsp;
on it, so take that as you will.

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:39.200
The TV mentions seven survivors found from the&nbsp;
Zoo Game of the last movie, but by my count we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.200 --> 00:02:44.640
only got six: Pamela Jenkins, Brent Abbot, Tara&nbsp;
Abbot, Addy, Emily and Shelby. Unless they’re&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:48.560
counting Jill Tuck, but she wasn’t actually&nbsp;
part of that game so… I don’t really get that.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:52.480
Another point I mentioned about the location&nbsp;
in the last video is that the TV station&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.480 --> 00:02:56.640
starts with W, which signifies a channel&nbsp;
in the Eastern United States, whereas K&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.640 --> 00:03:02.160
means Western states. The TV station where Bobby&nbsp;
Dagen has his talk show appearance is KWGA-TV,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.160 --> 00:03:04.160
so I think he filmed the interview in another city&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:07.760
shortly before traveling back across the&nbsp;
country for the survivor support group.

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:10.720
The host actually does mention&nbsp;
that he’s on a national tour.

00:03:10.720 --> 00:03:15.440
“Welcome back. We’re live with&nbsp;
Bobby Dagen, whose national tour,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.440 --> 00:03:20.560
S.U.R.V.I.V.E: My Story of Overcoming&nbsp;
JIGSAW, rolls into town this weekend.”

00:03:20.560 --> 00:03:24.400
She’s also got a buddha statue on her set,&nbsp;
just like the one seen by John’s deathbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:27.920
in Saw 3. But what’s more interesting&nbsp;
about this talk show is the title of it:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.920 --> 00:03:30.240
Daybreak with Donna Evans. The previous year,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.240 --> 00:03:34.480
there were a number of horror movies distributed&nbsp;
by Lionsgate, one of which was called Daybreakers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.480 --> 00:03:39.834
which was a pretty good vampire movie with&nbsp;
one part that just… I’m gonna roll the clip.

00:03:53.387 --> 00:03:55.387
*hilarious scream*

00:03:56.071 --> 00:03:57.520
*laughing*&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.520 --> 00:04:00.880
Another thing that’s very interesting&nbsp;
about this, and completely unplanned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.880 --> 00:04:04.240
is that Daybreakers was directed by Peter&nbsp;
and Michael Spierig, who would eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:09.360
be the ones to bring the Saw series back from&nbsp;
the dead, pun intended, in 2017 with JIGSAW.

00:04:09.360 --> 00:04:14.240
This is the point where Saw 7 does another&nbsp;
thing the series has never done before and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.240 --> 00:04:18.320
shows us a dream sequence, which I’m not that&nbsp;
crazy about because the series has been kind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:22.080
of grounded in reality for the first 6&nbsp;
entries, so why are we doing this now?

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:25.200
What I do find interesting about this&nbsp;
trap, which is called The Pain Train,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.200 --> 00:04:29.520
is it’s possible connection to Spiral. We see&nbsp;
the signature Jigsaw spiral on this device,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:33.280
and although this is just a nightmare,&nbsp;
maybe Jill had this bad dream based off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.280 --> 00:04:36.880
a real trap idea that she found in&nbsp;
his workshop. Spiral’s not out yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:40.240
but based on the trailer, it does look&nbsp;
like there’s something involving a train.

00:04:40.240 --> 00:04:42.240
I’m also glad they brought back the METAL for this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:42.240 --> 00:04:44.480
scene. We haven’t had a good&nbsp;
metal Saw trap in a while.

00:04:44.480 --> 00:04:51.600
🎵 Metal Music 🎵

00:04:51.600 --> 00:04:54.320
Also, I can’t show it for&nbsp;
multiple obvious reasons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:54.320 --> 00:04:58.240
but after she’s torn in half by The Pain Train,&nbsp;
you can just see her boobs for half a second.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:01.920
Just hanging there on opposite sides. Didn’t&nbsp;
notice that the first couple times I watched it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:05.280
but feel free to pop the Blu-Ray&nbsp;
if that’s your thing, I guess.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.320
Last episode I pointed out how she still&nbsp;
had pictures of John in her apartment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.320 --> 00:05:11.520
which could be used to explain why she was&nbsp;
willing to carry out his plan, because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.520 --> 00:05:15.040
looks like there are still some feelings there.&nbsp;
She’s got a shot of him by her bedside too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:19.280
and she still sleeps on one side of the bed. This&nbsp;
movie is all about her trying to stay safe from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.920
Hoffman but, I don’t think wine and pills are the&nbsp;
best way to go when you’re trying to stay alive.

00:05:23.920 --> 00:05:26.800
After this, there’s a time skip&nbsp;
forward to the next Jigsaw trap,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.600
and the main victim is this guy&nbsp;
named Evan. If he looks familiar,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.800
it’s because he’s played by Linkin Park&nbsp;
frontman Chester Bennington. Apparently&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.800 --> 00:05:37.120
one of the guys in his band lived next door to&nbsp;
Saw producer Mark Burg and the rest is history.

00:05:37.120 --> 00:05:39.600
Evan and his friends would not&nbsp;
make it out of this trap though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:43.040
and so, would not get to participate&nbsp;
in the Jigsaw Survivors Support Group,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.040 --> 00:05:46.960
which contains a whole bunch of cameos from&nbsp;
characters who survived in movies past,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.960 --> 00:05:51.840
and many of them look different than&nbsp;
they did during their initial tests.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:03.920
The Jigsaw Survivor Support Group&nbsp;
brings together those who managed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:07.760
to escape their tests dating all the&nbsp;
way back from the first Saw to Saw 7.

00:06:07.760 --> 00:06:11.120
They include Lawrence Gordon, the&nbsp;
doctor from The Bathroom Game in Saw;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.120 --> 00:06:14.336
Scott Mallick, the arsonist of the Fatal&nbsp;
Five in Saw 5;

00:06:14.336 --> 00:06:17.481
Simone, the loan shark from the Pound of Flesh Trap in Saw 6;

00:06:17.481 --> 00:06:20.034
Addy, the&nbsp;
receptionist of Umbrella Health in Saw 6;

00:06:20.034 --> 00:06:23.394
Emily, one of the policy reviewers in from&nbsp;
Umbrella Health in Saw 6;

00:06:23.394 --> 00:06:25.710
Tara Abbott, the widowed mother in Saw 6;

00:06:25.710 --> 00:06:27.858
Brad and Ryan&nbsp;
from the opening of Saw 7;

00:06:27.858 --> 00:06:30.650
Bobby Dagen, who was only pretending to be a victim to sell&nbsp;
books

00:06:30.650 --> 00:06:32.140
and a new character named Sidney.

00:06:32.140 --> 00:06:36.472
There are a couple of other new characters as well,&nbsp;
though we never learn their names or stories.

00:06:36.472 --> 00:06:43.037
There are other known survivors who are&nbsp;
missing from this group. From Saw 1 we have Jeff Ridenhour, Alison Gordon and Diana Gordon.

00:06:43.037 --> 00:06:44.801
From Saw 2, there’s Daniel Matthews.

00:06:44.801 --> 00:06:46.247
From Saw 3, there’s Corbett Denlon.

00:06:46.247 --> 00:06:48.621
From Saw 4, there’s&nbsp;
the teacher’s wife, Morgan.

00:06:48.621 --> 00:06:50.692
From Saw 5, we have Brit Stevenson.

00:06:50.692 --> 00:06:53.845
From Saw 6 there’s Brent&nbsp;
Abbot, Pamela Jenkins and Shelby.

00:06:53.895 --> 00:06:56.681
From Saw 7, there’s Joan, the woman who inspired Bobby&nbsp;
Dagen.

00:06:56.681 --> 00:06:58.717
And from Jigsaw, there’s Logan Nelson.

00:06:58.717 --> 00:07:00.377
We get to see the story of the new character, 

00:07:00.377 --> 00:07:04.400
Sidney. The reason she is tested is not&nbsp;
clear, but the theme of the trap seems to be…&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:09.600
I don’t know, gardening themed? We’ve got&nbsp;
lawnmowers, potted plants, some Keemstar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:12.960
merch. You really gonna start killing people&nbsp;
for not mowing the dang old lawn Jigsaw?

00:07:12.960 --> 00:07:17.040
She gets into it a little bit with the one-handed&nbsp;
wonder, Simone. We saw her in the last movie&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:21.040
explode at Hoffman for asking if she learned&nbsp;
anything from Jigsaw’s test, and this time she&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.040 --> 00:07:24.800
blows up on Sidney for suggesting that the trap&nbsp;
was the best thing that ever happened to her.

00:07:26.880 --> 00:07:34.160
“That’s a bunch of bull****. He had to die for&nbsp;
you to leave him? You want to know the best thing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:43.517
that happened to me after having to cut off my&nbsp;
own arm? Is handicap parking at the DAMN MALL.”

00:07:43.517 --> 00:07:45.396
*clapping*

00:07:45.396 --> 00:07:51.520
Good job Simone.&nbsp;
Girl always stands her ground. The location where the group meets is inside&nbsp;
of a church. I’ve talked in the previous&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:54.960
videos about the religious undertones&nbsp;
of Jigsaw’s following. The survivors&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:58.640
of his tests become his followers, and his&nbsp;
rules serve as his commandments to them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.640 --> 00:08:02.400
So them meeting inside of a church is&nbsp;
an interesting decision in that respect.

00:08:02.400 --> 00:08:06.400
And I know I’ve said it before, but Gordon’s&nbsp;
part is so hilarious. I can’t not play it.

00:08:08.400 --> 00:08:15.520
“I’m sure I speak for everyone here when I&nbsp;
say how grateful we are to be part of your&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.520 --> 00:08:18.348
promotional DVD.”

00:08:18.348 --> 00:08:21.740
*clapping*

00:08:21.840 --> 00:08:26.000
So the Metropolitan Police Department couldn’t&nbsp;
get it done, the FBI couldn’t get it done, so now,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.000 --> 00:08:30.240
with the new Jigsaw being Hoffman, a police&nbsp;
officer, it falls on Internal Affairs,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:30.240 --> 00:08:34.160
the division that investigates crimes committed&nbsp;
by the police. Which raises the question,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.160 --> 00:08:37.360
who investigates IA? Because I&nbsp;
have a crime to report: Gibson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.360 --> 00:08:40.560
you made this way too obvious. Did&nbsp;
you have to make it that obvious?

00:08:40.560 --> 00:08:45.120
The main game of Saw 7 follows Bobby Dagen as he&nbsp;
tries to save those close to him as punishment&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.120 --> 00:08:49.360
for faking that he was a Jigsaw victim. His&nbsp;
wife watches his struggle on the monitor.

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:52.800
“What’s going on? WHAT’S GOING OOONNNN??!”

00:08:52.800 --> 00:08:56.080
Like, do you really not know? Your&nbsp;
husband wrote a book all about this,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.080 --> 00:08:57.920
do you seriously not know
what’s going on here?

00:08:57.920 --> 00:09:00.160
We see the moment Bobby had the idea to write his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.160 --> 00:09:03.600
book after seeing the story of&nbsp;
a survivor of one of the traps.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:13.360
“It gave me light. That pure moment of absolute&nbsp;
horror gave me light and as wrong as it may seem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.800 --> 00:09:16.720
I’m better for enduring it.”

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:20.240
It’s interesting the way she words it,&nbsp;
that it gave her light, because John talks&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.240 --> 00:09:23.760
about the idea of enlightenment for this&nbsp;
test subjects from the very first movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:26.720
and lights were used as a symbol of&nbsp;
this at the beginning of each film.

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.120
As Bobby continues down the IKEA path, he&nbsp;
discovers his own motivational phrases being&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:31.120 --> 00:09:35.120
mockingly displayed against him. We know this&nbsp;
because the title of his book is an acronym,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:35.120 --> 00:09:39.200
S.U.R.V.I.V.E. We can’t really see the text on the&nbsp;
book cover, but if we take the first letter of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.200 --> 00:09:43.280
phrases graffitied on the psychiatric hospital&nbsp;
in the order they appear, it spells out the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:43.280 --> 00:09:48.160
word survive. Put it all together and you get:&nbsp;
START your life anew, UNDERSTAND your problems,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:48.160 --> 00:09:51.520
REDEFINE your priorities, VERIFY&nbsp;
your self worth through commitment,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:56.240
IGNORE your detractors, VALUE your loved&nbsp;
ones, EMBRACE every day as if it is your last.

00:09:56.240 --> 00:10:00.240
There’s also a theme that connects each room&nbsp;
throughout the game. Bobby’s associates enable&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:04.000
his behaviour by knowingly supporting his lie.&nbsp;
Throughout the series, there have been many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.000 --> 00:10:08.080
examples I’ve pointed out of John pulling ideas&nbsp;
from Asian culture, and the traps in this game&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.080 --> 00:10:12.880
connect to a Japanese legend known as The Three&nbsp;
Wise Monkeys, a 17th century pictorial maxim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.880 --> 00:10:16.800
meant to teach its audience not to ignore when&nbsp;
they see someone commit a sin. The three monkeys&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:16.800 --> 00:10:22.480
are Mizaru, translating to “see not”; Kikazaru,&nbsp;
which means “hear not” and Iwazaru, “speak not”.

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:25.440
In the tape for each of the games in&nbsp;
Bobby’s trial, Jigsaw mentions that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:30.240
his associates must speak no evil, hear&nbsp;
no evil or see no evil in order to pass.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:30.240 --> 00:10:32.720
The first room contains a&nbsp;
trap for his publicist Nina.

00:10:34.800 --> 00:10:42.080
“She has been your publicist for years, knowing&nbsp;
your lies but choosing to speak no evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:42.080 --> 00:10:49.360
She has been richly rewarded for her words, but&nbsp;
today she will be rewarded for her silence.”

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:53.120
And the trap literally threatens her ability&nbsp;
to speak, and help spread Bobby’s lies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.120 --> 00:10:57.120
by threatening to impale her throat if she makes&nbsp;
too much noise before he retrieves the key.

00:10:57.120 --> 00:11:00.800
This is one of the most frustrating traps&nbsp;
ever, because she hears the tape too,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:00.800 --> 00:11:03.120
she knows she’s not supposed to scream, and yet…

00:11:03.120 --> 00:11:08.000
*SCREAMING*

00:11:08.000 --> 00:11:11.760
I mean is she that dumb? Does she have a&nbsp;
deathwish? Why does she keep screaming?

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:14.160
“Why wouldn’t you just shut the ****&nbsp;
up? You just needed to shut up!”

00:11:14.160 --> 00:11:16.400
Thank you! Nina was the dumbest victim ever!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.400 --> 00:11:19.040
The next room contains a trap&nbsp;
for Bobby’s lawyer, Suzanne.

00:11:21.200 --> 00:11:25.920
“Before you is your lawyer, who&nbsp;
swore an oath of ethics but chose&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.920 --> 00:11:29.680
to see no evil in order to benefit herself.”

00:11:29.680 --> 00:11:32.480
If Bobby could not save her, she&nbsp;
would be forced to see no evil,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.480 --> 00:11:36.000
because she’d be impaled in the face with more&nbsp;
spikes, probably making her look like a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:36.000 --> 00:11:40.240
Minecraft creeper. And like the last game, why&nbsp;
is she screaming through the entire thing? The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:40.240 --> 00:11:43.680
dude’s obviously trying to help you, just&nbsp;
shut up for a minute and let him focus.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.960
I’m having a hard time feeling bad for&nbsp;
anyone who’s died in this movie so far.

00:11:46.960 --> 00:11:51.840
The third room, by default is the “hear no evil”&nbsp;
room, and contains Bobby’s best friend Cale.

00:11:51.840 --> 00:11:57.760
“Across from you is your closest friend. He&nbsp;
knows all your sins, but he acts as though&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:05.200
he hears no evil. Today what he hears will&nbsp;
be the difference between life and death.”

00:12:05.200 --> 00:12:08.560
So Bobby has to guide him across the&nbsp;
room using only audible commands.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:08.560 --> 00:12:12.720
I mean I would have just told him to stay put,&nbsp;
gotten on all fours and taken the key to him,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:12.720 --> 00:12:17.280
which would have been way faster and safer but…&nbsp;
I’m starting to think Bobby’s not the smartest.

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:20.320
But as this game is going on,&nbsp;
the search for Hoffman continues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.320 --> 00:12:23.840
and while this email sent to Gibson’s&nbsp;
office only appears on screen momentarily,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:25.920
there is a lot of info we can pull from it.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:42.320
Hoffman sends a DVD to Gibson’s supposedly&nbsp;
secret safehouse. The disk is named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.880 --> 00:12:48.720
Sc57_VHS_edit_4x3. I wish I could say this was a&nbsp;
callback to the first movie where we saw the label&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:48.720 --> 00:12:53.520
that says “Saw bathroom set”, and that by doing&nbsp;
this they are bringing the series somehow full circle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:53.520 --> 00:12:56.960
but this is kind of inexcusable seven&nbsp;
films in seven years type of stuff.

00:12:56.960 --> 00:12:58.960
With the location of the safehouse compromised,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.960 --> 00:13:01.840
Gibson decides to move Jill to a&nbsp;
holding cell in the police station for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:01.840 --> 00:13:05.840
safety. He comes in to ask her a question,&nbsp;
interrupting her reading this blank book.

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:08.640
“Do you know this guy?”

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:13.040
Hoffman sends another video, this time over email,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:13.040 --> 00:13:15.920
and if we pause on this screen&nbsp;
there are a few things to note.

00:13:15.920 --> 00:13:20.240
The most recent email is obviously from Detective&nbsp;
Hoffman, but the one before that is from Sorin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:20.240 --> 00:13:24.160
Popescu. Those of you who’ve been following&nbsp;
all my Things You Missed in Saw episodes will&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:27.200
remember that Sorin is the first assistant&nbsp;
art director of these movies, but his name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:27.200 --> 00:13:31.440
has appeared as an easter egg in Saw 5 and Saw&nbsp;
6, so I’ve been piecing together a backstory for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:31.440 --> 00:13:35.520
him. Based on the previous appearances of his&nbsp;
name, we determined he was a doctor from Norway&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:35.520 --> 00:13:39.920
who was a victim of a crime while visiting the&nbsp;
US. Seeing as how he’s now sending files to Matt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:39.920 --> 00:13:43.760
Gibson of Internal Affairs, I would have to say&nbsp;
the crime committed against him must have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:43.760 --> 00:13:48.160
committed by the police. Maybe he was subject&nbsp;
to some of Eric Matthew’s police “brutality.”

00:13:48.160 --> 00:13:51.680
The other guy emailing Gibson is Brent&nbsp;
McGillivray. This is the first time we’ve&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.680 --> 00:13:55.360
seen that name and he’s credited as a first&nbsp;
assistant art director and set designer.

00:13:55.360 --> 00:13:57.840
In the other column here we can&nbsp;
see Hoffman’s email address. Now&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:57.840 --> 00:14:01.600
I know there is a lot of debate in the Saw&nbsp;
fandom on whether or not Hoffman is alive,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:01.600 --> 00:14:03.760
so why don’t we just send&nbsp;
him an email and ask him?

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:08.320
To: m hoffman @ metro dot pol
 
Mark, I just wanted to email you to confirm&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.320 --> 00:14:13.520
whether or not you are, in fact, still alive.&nbsp;
Please email me back if you are not dead. Thanks,
 &nbsp;

00:14:13.520 --> 00:14:17.200
I’ll let you guys know if he gets back to me.&nbsp;
Below Hoffman’s email address, we have the date:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.200 --> 00:14:22.160
March 12th. Saw 5 and Saw 6 took place in July&nbsp;
2006, and we can assume Jill’s dream took place&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:26.640
in the winter on account of all the snow, so&nbsp;
the March 12th date puts us in early 2007,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:26.640 --> 00:14:30.640
which checks out nicely with the 2007&nbsp;
calendar we saw at the auto body shop.

00:14:30.640 --> 00:14:34.080
Under that we see Gibson’s email address.&nbsp;
Might as well send him something as well.

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:36.720
M gibson @ metro dot pol

00:14:36.720 --> 00:14:40.240
Hey Gibson, I know you're now dead, but&nbsp;
I just wanted to say, you were the only&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:40.240 --> 00:14:45.040
good cop besides Kerry, and she's also&nbsp;
dead. Rest in peace man, rest in peace.

00:14:45.040 --> 00:14:47.200
The email content reminds Gibson of an event&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.200 --> 00:14:50.960
from their past where Hoffman saved his&nbsp;
life by mercilessly shooting a junkie.

00:14:50.960 --> 00:14:53.920
“I had no choice but to report&nbsp;
Hoffman for “Brutality.”

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:57.680
They end up finding the secret room in the&nbsp;
auto body shop behind the bathroom mirror. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.680 --> 00:15:01.520
might be a reference to Candyman, where the two&nbsp;
apartments are connected by a mirror in a similar&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:01.520 --> 00:15:06.160
fashion, but could also just be a callback to&nbsp;
Saw 5 and the hidden doorway seen in that movie.

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:09.360
They end up finding Hoffman and John’s notes&nbsp;
about the victims for this game, and they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.360 --> 00:15:13.840
give us a little bit more insight into how John’s&nbsp;
assistants are always able to find and kidnap the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:13.840 --> 00:15:18.480
vicitms so easily. There are detailed notes about&nbsp;
their daily routines, their personal information,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:18.480 --> 00:15:22.480
where they can be found; even specifics about&nbsp;
their alarm codes and computer passwords.

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:26.320
Bobby’s final test is to do what he lied&nbsp;
about having done in his book by impaling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:30.880
his pecs with meat hooks and hoisting himself&nbsp;
up. This has got to be the dumbest trap yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.880 --> 00:15:34.560
Just put your feet on the smooth part of&nbsp;
these hooks and pull yourself up that way.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:34.560 --> 00:15:38.160
Or just climb the chain or something. This is&nbsp;
literally the dumbest way to go about this.

00:15:38.160 --> 00:15:42.480
Naturally, he fails, and as a result, his&nbsp;
wife has to pay the price for reason. It&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.480 --> 00:15:45.440
doesn’t really say why she was a part&nbsp;
of this, but I think we can assume it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:45.440 --> 00:15:48.880
has something to do with the fact that she&nbsp;
was only interested in him for his fame.

00:15:48.880 --> 00:15:53.600
Her punishment is a modern day version of The&nbsp;
Brazen Bull, a torture device / execution chamber&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:53.600 --> 00:15:57.440
depicted in renderings from Ancient Greece in&nbsp;
which a person was placed inside of a hollowed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:57.440 --> 00:16:01.680
bronze bull and a fire was ignited underneath,&nbsp;
thereby roasting the person, but not the way&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:01.680 --> 00:16:05.760
I might roast my opponents in Rocket League,&nbsp;
but actually roasting them, like the way you&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:10.560
might roast your shwarma before sticking it in a&nbsp;
Pita and drizzling over tahini sauce, to stay on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.476
the Mediteranean theme of this trap.

00:16:12.476 --> 00:16:15.666
The Saw version of the Brazen Bull seems to be more shaped like a Brazen Pig, 

00:16:15.666 --> 00:16:19.657
keeping up with the theme of pigs that has come up time and time again in this franchise. 

00:16:19.657 --> 00:16:21.760
Like the&nbsp;
Pendulum Trap from from the fifth movie, there

00:16:21.760 --> 00:16:26.320
is some debate as to whether or not the Brazen&nbsp;
Bull actually ended up becoming a real thing.

00:16:26.320 --> 00:16:29.120
And that’s the last we see of&nbsp;
Bobby Dagen. I guess he’s most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:29.120 --> 00:16:32.160
likely still alive. I’d personally&nbsp;
like to see him write another book.

00:16:32.160 --> 00:16:35.280
The police realize that Hoffman&nbsp;
tapped into their security system.

00:16:35.280 --> 00:16:36.960
“He’s been watching us the whole time.”

00:16:36.960 --> 00:16:39.520
I think it’s not so much that&nbsp;
he’s a hacker, but more that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:42.880
he’s a cop and I guess they forgot&nbsp;
to revoke his access after realizing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:42.880 --> 00:16:46.000
he’s bad. Or maybe he actually is a&nbsp;
hacker, who even knows at this point?

00:16:46.000 --> 00:16:49.920
Hoffman had hidden himself in one of the body&nbsp;
bags being transported back to the station,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:49.920 --> 00:16:53.360
and the first person he encounters is the&nbsp;
coroner. This is meaningful for Hoffman,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:53.360 --> 00:16:56.800
because this was the same guy that discovered&nbsp;
that a different knife was used to cut the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:56.800 --> 00:17:00.000
jigsaw piece from Seth Baxter, which is&nbsp;
what led to Hoffman getting caught by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:00.000 --> 00:17:03.280
the FBI in the previous movie, forcing&nbsp;
him to hide out for 8 months.

00:17:03.280 --> 00:17:08.240
He then goes on a rampage, taking out nearly the&nbsp;
entire police station in pursuit of Jill Tuck.

00:17:08.240 --> 00:17:09.520
“I’ve been looking for you.”

00:17:09.520 --> 00:17:14.000
If we pause as Jill grabs something to defend&nbsp;
herself with, it looks to be part of a nail file&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:14.000 --> 00:17:17.760
kit. There’s also an article about lowering&nbsp;
the cost of healthcare by moving to Canada,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.760 --> 00:17:20.880
though I guess that would be&nbsp;
more appropriate in Saw 6.

00:17:20.880 --> 00:17:24.800
She’s able to get away, if only temporarily,&nbsp;
and scrambles to hide in the evidence room,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:24.800 --> 00:17:27.680
and in the background of nearly&nbsp;
every shot in there we can spot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.680 --> 00:17:32.240
old traps, newspaper headlines, design&nbsp;
sketches and more Things You Missed.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:47.040
After following the journeys of countless law&nbsp;
enforcement officers in their conquest to put an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.040 --> 00:17:51.440
end to the Jigsaw killings, it is only fitting&nbsp;
that we near the conclusion of the Saw saga&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:51.440 --> 00:17:55.920
by finally paying a visit to the main evidence&nbsp;
room, but fans may choose to see it as more of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:55.920 --> 00:18:00.400
a shrine to almost everything we have come to&nbsp;
know over the course of this 7 movie journey.

00:18:00.400 --> 00:18:04.080
As Hoffman comes in and turns on the lights&nbsp;
there is a collection of newspaper clippings&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.080 --> 00:18:08.080
and magazine covers, including this&nbsp;
one, Police Injured During Jigsaw Raid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:08.080 --> 00:18:12.320
which details the beginning of the invasion at&nbsp;
Wilson Steel Plant from the beginning of Saw 2.

00:18:12.320 --> 00:18:16.080
On the top shelf of this thing you’ll see the&nbsp;
Head Cage that we saw in John’s workshop in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:16.080 --> 00:18:20.320
Saw 2, but was never used. From that same&nbsp;
workshop, is this photo on the back wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.320 --> 00:18:24.560
which corresponds to the face mask trap that&nbsp;
I theorized to possibly be an early version of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:29.040
the Oxygen Crusher. And this giant clamp was&nbsp;
the device that crushed the janitor, Hank.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.040 --> 00:18:32.560
Just imagine the whole thing rotated so&nbsp;
the clamps are reaching out towards us.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:32.560 --> 00:18:35.680
Up in the corner may be the&nbsp;
oxygen tanks used in that trap.

00:18:35.680 --> 00:18:39.680
Hoffman also sees a mannequin from the&nbsp;
mannequin factory on Stygian Street in Saw 1,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.680 --> 00:18:44.000
where John narrowly evaded Sing and Tapp. Another&nbsp;
mannequin is seen on the other side of the room.

00:18:44.000 --> 00:18:48.560
There’s also a cart with the scale used in The&nbsp;
Pound of Flesh Trap, the opener from Saw 6.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:51.920
The headgear from The Pound of Flesh is sitting&nbsp;
on a shelf on the other side of the room, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:51.920 --> 00:18:56.000
the tubes they had to send their offerings down&nbsp;
to reach the scale are sitting behind the desk.

00:18:56.000 --> 00:19:00.080
Just beyond the scale is the Hydrofluoric&nbsp;
Acid injection rack from the Rowan Zoological&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:00.080 --> 00:19:04.560
Institute, also from Saw 6. Also from the zoo,&nbsp;
is the little cage that William Easton had to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.560 --> 00:19:09.360
put his hand into to determine who lives and who&nbsp;
dies during the infamous Shotgun Carousel Trap.

00:19:09.360 --> 00:19:11.920
These big gears appear to be&nbsp;
the components that held up the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:11.920 --> 00:19:15.200
giant ice blocks that eventually&nbsp;
crushed Eric Matthews in Saw 4.

00:19:15.200 --> 00:19:18.560
Over to the right, behind the wall we&nbsp;
can see part of The Rack from Saw 3.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:18.560 --> 00:19:21.360
In front of that wall is a&nbsp;
cage containing four shotguns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:21.360 --> 00:19:24.800
these are the Quadruple Shotguns&nbsp;
that got Detective Sing in Saw 1.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:28.400
The chair that Hoffman grabs is fittingly,&nbsp;
his chair from the end of the last movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:28.400 --> 00:19:30.640
that Jill setup to shock him when he sat down.

00:19:30.640 --> 00:19:35.680
Of course, who could possibly forget the good old&nbsp;
classics? Hoffman passes on the Reverse Bear Trap&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:35.680 --> 00:19:40.400
2.0 that Jill left him to die in at the end of the&nbsp;
previous movie and opts for the Saw 1 original.

00:19:40.400 --> 00:19:44.000
On the left wall there are more articles&nbsp;
and evidence photos including a very&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:44.000 --> 00:19:47.280
dapper looking shot of Yung Billy when&nbsp;
he was doing the whole Pete Wentz look,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:47.280 --> 00:19:51.440
which is right above the photo of John and Art&nbsp;
Blank at the opening of Gideon Meat Packing Plant.

00:19:51.440 --> 00:19:54.240
If you look all the way on the right,&nbsp;
partially obscured by the wall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:57.760
you’ll see the Water Cube Trap that&nbsp;
almost drowned Agent Strahm in Saw 5.

00:19:57.760 --> 00:20:02.000
I’m not entirely sure about this, because the&nbsp;
lighting is different, but the device seen on top&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:06.000
of the cart on the right here may be the spinal&nbsp;
contraption that Art Blank had to wear during his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.000 --> 00:20:10.080
test in Saw 4, and these wheels could really be&nbsp;
a number of things, but they may be the device&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:10.080 --> 00:20:14.240
John used to manipulate the audio in this&nbsp;
tapes, we see it in a flashback in Saw 4.

00:20:14.240 --> 00:20:19.040
So that’s the evidence room, and that’s the&nbsp;
Saw saga. I do feel like I may not have gotten&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:19.040 --> 00:20:22.560
every little thing, so let me know if you&nbsp;
caught any other references in the comments.

00:20:22.560 --> 00:20:26.640
The Saw series was thought to be over&nbsp;
after the release of Saw 7 in 2010,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:26.640 --> 00:20:30.640
but like many horror icons, Jigsaw&nbsp;
could not be kept off the silver screen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.640 --> 00:20:35.280
and the franchise would return with Jigsaw in&nbsp;
2017. Click the video on the left to see what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.280 --> 00:20:38.960
you might have missed in that eighth installment,&nbsp;
and be sure to subscribe, because I think it may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:38.960 --> 00:20:43.520
be time to give that video a revisit and offer up&nbsp;
a few new Things You Missed. Make sure you ring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:43.520 --> 00:20:48.640
the deathbell for notifications and I’ll see&nbsp;
you in the next one. Assuming we both survive.

00:20:51.280 --> 00:20:54.720
Hoffman never emailed me back so… guess he’s dead.

